## Introduction
More than 100 years ago, Emil Fisher ‘foresaw a time in which physiological chemistry will not only make greater use of natural enzymes, but will actually resort to creating synthetic ones’ [1]. The field of $d e$ novo design has established the principles of assembly of water-soluble $\mathfrak{a}$ -helical bundles [2] and basic $\upbeta$ -sheet proteins [3] and today can create unique 3D structures $[4,5^{\bullet},6,7^{\bullet\bullet},8^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . Yet it is still a challenge to readily translate any particular protein function into a synthetic protein construction.
This difficulty has as much to do with the complexity of natural proteins, as with our incomplete understanding of the chemical and physical mechanisms of the more inspiring activities in biology. A major source of this complexity, illustrated on the amino acid scale by Figure 1, is Darwin’s principle of multiple-utility: each amino acid in a native protein is naturally selected for a multiplicity of overlapping and concurrent roles. The continuing challenge in de novo design and synthesis of proteins is to purposely cut through this complexity to focus on one or two roles and, in a modular fashion, progressively build up protein function. The relative robustness of many protein functions to the mutational changes experienced during natural evolution encourages us to recreate these functions in a vastly simplified framework that will demonstrate what properties are or are not essential for a particular function and use the acquired principles to fabricate modern nanoscale devices. In this commentary, we select biological energy conversion typically carried out by membrane-embedded proteins as the function of interest and outline the strategy for construction of simple and robust bioenergetic systems. The strategy has three stages: first, to abstract key functional elements from the membrane proteins and reproduce them in readily adaptable water-soluble maquettes; second, to provide a structural basis for the maquette properties; and third, to integrate these functional properties back into a membrane.
## Reproducing functional elements of membrane proteins in water-soluble maquettes
The main functional elements involved in biological energy transduction are conversion of light or chemical redox energy into electron transfer, coupling electron transfer to proton exchange, and coupling proton translocation with conformational change. These actions are invariably contained within membranes and directed vectorially across these membranes. The first stage towards making simple and robust bioenergetic systems has been established by reworking and regularizing the hydrophobic di-heme subunit of the membrane redox protein cytochrome $b c_{1}$ into a prototype hydrophilic (HP) four-helix heme-binding maquette [9]. Numerous redox maquettes have now been created, containing flavins [10], iron–sulfur clusters [11], chlorophylls [12], chlorins [13], redox-active tyrosine and tryptophan residues [6], carboxylate-bridged dimetal clusters [14–16] and various forms of heme [9,17]. The HP family of maquettes developed from the prototype proved to be readily adaptable and successfully reproduced key functional elements on the same energetic-, length- and time-scales as those found in natural proteins. These include electrostatic coupling between redox centers and between redox centers and
Much of the complexity of natural proteins comes from the multiple roles each amino acid plays. The simplicity of maquettes comes from intentionally reducing the roles played to a select few, emphasized here in green text.
proton transfer sites [18], allosterically regulated, chargeactivated conformational switching [19], and lightactivated electron transfer both within a maquette [10] and between maquette and electrode [20].
## Uncovering the structural basis for functional elements
Nothing can substitute for atomically resolved structures in understanding functional properties to guide future maquette design; yet resolving structures of redox maquettes has proven to be an uncertain exercise. The first hydrophilic de novo designed proteins relied on simple hydrophobic sequestration to drive the folding without specific inter-residue interactions, resulting in ‘molten globule’-like states in both the apo- and holoforms. Design of native-like singular structures has been approached by computational, combinatorial designdirected genetic methods $[5^{\bullet},21-23]$ and iterative redesign $[8^{\bullet\bullet},24,25]$ . The directed iterative redesign strategy has achieved structurally unique maquettes without loss of functional elements displayed by the maquette prototype [9], as illustrated in Figure 2 for diheme four- $\cdot\propto$ -helix protein maquettes. The same subunit of the natural cytochrome $b c_{1}$ that served as a model for the HP family prototype has also been successfully redesigned by computational methods into a water-soluble diheme fourhelix bundle protein that is uniquely structured with topology identical to the natural one (G Ghirlanda and WF Degrado, personal communication).
A further challenge is to obtain structural information when an amphiphilic maquette is organized at an interface, either in a membrane, an air/aqueous interface, selfassembled onto a solid semiconductor or electrode substrate as part of a device. X-ray/neutron scattering and polarized epifluorescence techniques have been applied to water-soluble maquettes, sequestered at the air/ aqueous interface by attaching palmitic acid to one end of the helices [26]. In this case, bundles assemble in a syn topology (all helices with the same orientation). By designing favorable electrostatic interactions between the polar faces of the amphipathic helices, maquettes with anti topology (di-helices in opposite orientation) are now also being characterized [27].
## Integrating the maquettes back to the membrane
The originally lipophilic (LP) transmembrane (TM) domain of cytochrome $b c_{I}$ was redesigned to be hydrophilic (HP) to significantly simplify preparation and functional assessment. However, charge separation and TM proton gradients, which are the fundamental energetic currency in natural systems, immediately dissipate without a charge-impermeable barrier. To make the transition from the aqueous environment back to the membrane, we follow the known principles of synthetic membrane protein assembly, draw inspiration from some of the important features of membrane proteins with known 3D structure, and proceed with the design of amphiphilic (AP) membrane-associating redox maquettes.
## Principles of synthetic membrane protein assembly Satisfy the H-bonding
During assembly, proteins that have a substantial hydrophobic interior or a substantial component inside a membrane need to significantly reduce the energetic cost of transferring the polar peptide bonds from water into the hydrophobic interior $(\sim1.2$ to $0.7\mathrm{kcal/mol}$ per residue) [28]. This is done by forming secondary structural elements that enable peptide bonds to participate in H-bonding. The H-bonding is most easily established internally via formation of $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices, although it can be also accomplished with $\upbeta\cdot$ -sheets provided that the $\upbeta$ -strands form closed structures such as a $\upbeta$ -barrel. Hydrophilic maquettes described here begin with such an $\mathsf{\Omega}\propto$ - helical structure.
## Sink the $\pmb{\alpha}$ -helix
Once the secondary structure is formed, the process of membrane protein assembly involves insertion of the secondary structure elements into a membrane [29]. Investigations of single $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helix insertion into membranes started with the simplest of sequences such as ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{L}}_{24}{\mathrm{K}}_{2}$ [30] with two charged amino acids terminating a hydrophobic series long enough to span the membrane (Figure 3a). Systematic studies revealed what sort of hydrophobic residue substitutions can be tolerated, how the length of the peptide affects the peptide–lipid assembly, and what energetic cost is involved in peptide– lipid interactions [31]. For example, length variation of peptides of the form acetyl-GWW $\mathrm{(LA)_{x}L}$ WWA-ethanolamine and acetyl-GKK(LA) LKKA-amide, (where x is varied between 5 and 8) $[32.-34,35^{\bullet}]$ , and comparison of different interfacially important amino acids revealed how peptides adapt to a membrane–water interface. Hydrophobic mismatch between the peptide and bilayer can be relieved by peptide tilt [31] or diminished by the special energetic tolerance of aromatic residues for the interfacial region [32,33]. Charged residues with long side chains (such as lysine) can override hydrophobic mismatch by ‘snorkeling’ into the headgroup region [34] with relatively minor $(0.07{-}0.7\ \mathrm{kcal/mol})$ energetic cost $[35^{\bullet}]$ .
Useful free energy scales for transferring amino acids between mixtures of water and octanol (octanol served as a substitute for the bilayer interior, despite the fact that it contains substantial amount of water $(2.3\mathrm{M})$ [36]) and between water and the bilayer interface have been determined from partition coefficients of small peptides of polar and non-polar residues not long enough to span the membrane [28,29,37,38]. These hydrophobicity scales became a benchmark for successful prediction of TM segments of proteins with known amino acids sequence [38], and gauge which TM $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -helices will insert spontaneously into membrane [39].
## Bundle the helices
Single $\mathfrak{a}$ -helical membrane units provide sufficient functionality if the goal is only to anchor a redox protein to the membrane, but specific helix–helix association into welldefined bundles is needed to achieve more advanced functional features such as selective vectorial transport of charged species across the membrane. The inter-helical interactions that stabilize membrane (as opposed to water soluble) segments are still not completely understood. The problem lies in the paucity of techniques to assess the nature and energetics of the interhelical TM domain association. Commonly used is analytical ultracentrifugation even though it requires solubilization of the peptide by detergent which, until recently [40], involved laborious density matching. An attractive alternative system to ascertain that peptides inserted within a membrane actually interact with each other has been developed using TOXCAT in the Escherichia coli inner membrane [41]. Both ends of the test TM domain are fused to ‘purposeful’ domains: the C-terminus to maltose binding protein, which preferentially localizes on the periplasmic side, and the N-terminus to DNA binding domain of ToxR. The ToxR domain is a transcriptional activator of a reporter gene encoding chloramphenicol acetyltransferase (CAT) that only works when the TM domains dimerize.
Thus, the level of CAT expression provides a clear measure of the dimerization. The homo-dimerization restriction of TOXCAT has been overcome with a new method (GALLEX) to establish a measure of heterologous association of TM units by using asymmetric promoter $[42^{\bullet}]$ .
First principles are emerging. Hydrogen-bonding interactions of just one polar residue per helix have sufficient thermodynamic driving force for membrane helix association [43–45]. However, the degree of stabilization is strongly dependent on the position: when asparagine is positioned in the middle of a membrane, the trimeric state is stabilized by at least $-2\mathrm{kcal/mol}$ per asparagine, whereas when placed near the interface no stabilization is observed $(0\pm0.5\mathrm{kcal/mol}$ per monomer) [46].
## Modularity in natural membrane proteins
Although the number of proteins with known 3D structure is steadily increasing $[47^{\bullet\bullet}]$ , the structure/function relationships of the several available redox-linked proton pumping membrane proteins is too uncertain for direct translation into maquettes. The simpler ion channels are more accessible, including those with recently solved 3D structures, such as ClC chloride channel [48], McsS mechanosensitive channel [49], the M2 proton channel from influenza A $[50^{\bullet}]$ , and the voltage [51] and $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ $[52^{\bullet\bullet}]$ gated $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ channels. A comparison of the structures of three differently gated potassium channels $[51,52^{\bullet\bullet}$ , 53,54] provides a clear example of how nature put intron swapping to work in the world of membrane proteins. The functional channel element is conserved in all three structures (Figure 3b (i)–(iii), orange domain), but channel gating is customized by specific ‘modules’ added either outside the membrane as in $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ gated channel (Figure 3b (ii) blue domain) or inside the membrane as in the voltage sensor in the KvAP channel (Figure 3b (iii), red domain). This modularity observed in natural protein constructs for a variety of functions endorses our principles of maquette design and constructions.
## Amphiphilic design of redox protein maquettes
With the main principles of membrane protein assembly and various redox, proton exchange and conformational maquette elements in hand, we can begin assembly of functional elements in membranes. There are at least two approaches that can be used together or separately. The first approach is to redesign the external residues of a particular module for solubility in the membrane [43]; the second is the above mentioned modular approach [55]. As seen in $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ channels, the LP functional modules can be combined with either HP or LP control modules.
To separate charge across a membrane we can use protein modules for either ion channels [56–58] and/or redox cofactor chains supporting directional electron transfer. The de novo designed LP proton channel of $\mathrm{LS}_{2}$ [56] and the M2 proton channel structure from influenza A $[50^{\bullet}]$ both have the advantage of structural simplicity and tetrameric assembly that assists modular construction with the structured tetrameric HP series supporting redox coupled proton exchange. TM electron transfer cofactor chains could be assembled from an HP maquette module with suitable modification of the external residues or, because the maquette originated from the LP di-heme subunit of cytochrome $b c_{1}$ , a module can be constructed based on the native hydrophobic $b c_{1}$ heme-binding motif (Figure 4a).
Several modular AP maquettes that combine functional HP modules with LP TM modules have been successfully constructed (Figure 4b). HP modules retain the redox-coupled proton exchange and strengthen four-helix bundle association. LP modules facilitate maquette incorporation into membranes. We can impose a single orientation of the functional maquettes by adding heme to the external aqueous phase. In such cases, $50\%$ of the maquettes bind heme, while $50\%$ with the HP module on the inside of vesicle apparently do not pick up heme and remain non-functional.
The primary principles of these AP maquette designs are very simple: first, the water solubility of the external residues along the helical axis has to be inverted in the HP and LP segments; and second, the redox cofactor binding sites (when present) have to be secured in the bundle interior. Other engineering principles are placement of aromatic residues at the air–water interface, and the presence of a limited number of polar residues within the interior of LP domain for stabilization of the particular structure. This AP family should serve as a prototype for new TM maquettes that will be capable of redox-coupled TM charge motion leading to a proton gradient and electric field generation (Figure 4c).
## Conclusion
Significant accomplishments in de novo design of HP redox protein maquettes have been made in both structural and functional avenues: heme maquettes with unique structure on the NMR time scale are being generated and several functional elements have been reproduced. At the same time, considerable wealth of information has been obtained with respect to structure and assembly of membrane proteins. Drawing on the progress in both of these diverse fields, a new family of redox maquettes has been designed. Maquettes from this AP family are four-helical membrane proteins with overall amphiphilic character, maintaining many of the functional properties that now have to be orchestrated to create simple and robust redox energy transducing machines.
